Intel", -0.1403237283229828], [" the", -2.2771244049072266]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.5265577435493469, "top": [["'s", -0.5265577435493469], [" Arc", -1.430816411972046]]}, {"t": " GPU", "lp": -0.19999852776527405, "top": [[" GPU", -0.19999852776527405], [" driver", -2.5478758811950684]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.7791675925254822, "top": [[" driver", -0.7791675925254822], [" architecture", -0.9878182411193848]]}, {"t": " architecture", "lp": -0.005236244294792414, "top": [[" architecture", -0.005236244294792414], [" configuration", -7.1932549476623535]]}, {"t": ",", "lp": -0.12001489847898483, "top": [[",", -0.12001489847898483], [".", -2.3005645275115967]]}, {"t": " but", "lp": -0.0005070064216852188, "top": [[" but", -0.0005070064216852188], [" and", -7.908389568328857]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.2001432627439499, "top": [[" the", -0.2001432627439499], [" these", -3.0265612602233887]]}, {"t": " exact", "lp": -0.29492059350013733, "top": [[" exact", -0.29492059350013733], [" specific", -2.1384360790252686]]}, {"t": " semantics", "lp": -0.16439975798130035, "top": [[" semantics", -0.16439975798130035], [" behavior", -2.7136056423187256]]}, {"t": " of", "lp": -0.16421900689601898, "top": [[" of", -0.16421900689601898], [" and", -1.972753643989563]]}, {"t": " these", "lp": -0.20059500634670258, "top": [[" these", -0.20059500634670258], [" CCS", -2.1613457202911377]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nCCS stands for **Compute and Copy Engine Scheduler**. These modes control how the GPU's compute and copy engines are scheduled, which significantly affects performance and behavior.\n\n## CCS Mode 1\nThis is the **default mode** for most workloads. It uses a **single scheduler** that handles both compute and copy operations. This mode prioritizes:\n- Simplicity and stability\n- Good general-purpose performance\n- Lower overhead for typical workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 2\nThis mode enables **separate scheduling** for compute and copy engines. Key characteristics:\n- Better **concurrency** between compute and copy operations\n- Improved performance for workloads that mix compute kernels with data transfers\n- More efficient utilization of the GPU when you have overlapping compute and memory operations\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\nMode 4 is typically the **performance/maximum throughput mode**. You would use it when:\n\n- **You're benchmarking or pushing for maximum performance**\n- Running **compute-heavy workloads** (AI/ML inference, scientific computing)\n- You want to **minimize scheduling overhead** and maximize GPU utilization\n- Testing scenarios where you've verified stability\n\n## Practical Guidance\n\n| Mode | Best For |\n|------|----------|\n| 1 | Default, general use, stability |\n| 2 | Mixed compute + copy workloads |\n| 4 | Maximum performance, benchmarking |\n\n## How to Set It\n\nYou can typically configure this via environment variables or driver parameters, such as:\n\n```bash\nexport I915_XE_CCS_MODE=4\n```\n\nOr through kernel module parameters depending on your setup.\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm providing this based on general knowledge of Intel's GPU driver architecture, but the exact semantics of these"}